{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "Sally and Vanessa Visit the Grand Canyon", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "A Natural Wonder", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 4, "type": "position"}, {"content": "scholar > Nature Around Us > Greetings From Alaska", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 14, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A2.1", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Accurately use information about travel and vacations in social situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Identifies general information in short written or spoken conversations.", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": "Identifies general information in short written or spoken conversations.", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": "Gives appropriate directions to places taking into account the type of interlocutor.", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": "Gives appropriate directions to places taking into account the type of interlocutor.", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": "Identifies general information in short written or spoken conversations.", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": "Identifies general information in short written or spoken conversations.", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Identifies general information in short written or spoken conversations.", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Gives appropriate directions to places taking into account the type of interlocutor.", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Articles", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the article <span class=\"italic\"><span class =\"strong\">a</span></span> before singular, countable nouns which begin with consonant sounds.</br></br>Examples:</br><ul><li><span class=\"italic\">He is a farmer.</span></li></ul><ul><li><span class=\"italic\">She doesn't own a motorcycle.</span></li></ul><ul><li><span class=\"italic\">I saw a colorful bird in Belize.</span></li></ul></br></br>You use the article <span class =\"strong\"><span class=\"italic\">an</span></span> before singular, countable nouns which begin with vowel sounds.</br></br>Examples:</br><ul><li><span class=\"italic\">He is an astronaut.</span></li></ul><ul><li><span class=\"italic\">Sally didn't get an invitation.</span></li></ul><ul><li><span class=\"italic\">I saw an eagle in the zoo.</span></li></ul>", "type": "describer"}, {"content": " ", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Pronouncing <span class=\"italic\">a</span> and <span class=\"italic\">an</span>", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "Normally, you pronounce consonant letters with a consonant sound, and vowel letters with a vowel sound.</br></br>There are some exceptions. The rule for <span class =\"strong\"><span class=\"italic\">a</span></span> or <span class =\"strong\"><span class=\"italic\">an</span></span> is still the same. You just need to think about the sound, not the word. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Examples:<br/><br/>  <ul><li><span class=\"italic\">a European city</span><br/> <span class=\"italic\">EU</span> sounds like the consonant sound at the beginning of the word <span class=\"italic\">you</span>. </li> <li> <span class=\"italic\">a one-day tour</span><br/> The sound at the beginning of <span class=\"italic\">ONE</span> sounds like the consonant sound at the beginning of the word <span class=\"italic\">wonder</span>. </li> <li><span class=\"italic\">an F1 car</span><br/> The letter <span class=\"italic\">F</span> starts with a vowel sound, like in the two first letters of the word <span class=\"italic\">ef</span>fort. </li> <li><span class=\"italic\">an hour</span><br/> <span class=\"italic\">H</span> is silent in this word, so it sounds like <span class=\"italic\">our</span>, which starts with a vowel sound.</li></ul>.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you 